.TITLE "wsl"

.DIV "DESCRIPTION"
WSL stands for __Windows Subsystem for Linux__.

It lets you run a Linux environment on Windows without needing a full virtual machine.
You can think of it as a way to use Linux tools from inside Windows.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
wsl

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**wsl** opens your default Linux environment from Windows.

Here, __wsl__ is the tool.
It starts the Linux shell that is installed on your system.

This is useful when you want to use Linux commands on a Windows machine.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
wsl ls

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**wsl ls** runs the __ls__ command inside Linux from Windows.

Here, __ls__ is the Linux command being run through WSL.

This is helpful when you want to work with Linux tools without leaving Windows.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
wsl --version

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**wsl --version** shows the version of WSL installed on your system.

Here, __--version__ is a flag that asks the program to print version details.

Use this when you want to check what WSL you are using.

.DIV "SUMMARY"
WSL lets Windows users run Linux tools and commands.

It is useful when you need Linux access without switching systems.
